Title: ZODIAC WARS: BLOODLINES
Episode: 103, "THE OLD STORY"
Credit: Draft 1 SHORT — PROPOSED, not approved
Author: Prepared for Ω SYD OMEGA 91717 STUDIOS GLOBAL under the founder's direction
Draft date: 2026-11-10
Notes: Cold open and three condensed acts, about 14 script pages of a 50-minute episode, plus a short tag. Season 1, gate SCHOLAR, the Pisces episode. It follows episode 102 (short draft, not approved) by about a week. Canon this draft respects: the Deep Archive is a cathedral of water with glass tanks the height of towers (the film); the old story's words ("Before there were twelve, there were four...") are the film's, as are "they chose the throne" and "It was only paused"; Ima calls Solenne "Grandmother" and Solenne calls her "little tide"; the Drowned dissolved when Solenne named the living, and its last words were "Memory so deep nothing surfaces"; Solenne's memory came back at the end of the film; Poseidon is a voice in the water only; "hold the other end" is used once here and not again this season. New in this draft, and the founder's to accept or strike: that the Archive's catalogue begins at Year One of the Accord; the founding rule every archivist swears in a dead tongue, and its meaning; that the Drowned was that rule pursued without its limit; the dry hall under the tanks; and the shelf with no labels. Phaedra Mir, the registry's proposed Age IV regent of Pisces, is named but does not appear. No new named character. "Say the cost" is said once, by Ima. Nothing here is filmed, released or playable.

FADE IN:

INT. THE DEEP ARCHIVE, NEPTUNE — THE CATALOGUE — NIGHT

Blue water-light. At the foot of the great tanks stands the catalogue: a wall of brass drawers forty feet high, each no bigger than a hand, reached by a ladder on a rail. Thousands of them. Each has a small engraved plate.

Halfway up the ladder, in a nightshirt and one sock, with a lantern hooked over her arm: IMA, eight. She is reading the plates aloud, working along a row toward the far left edge.

IMA
Year nine. Year eight. Year seven.

She pulls the ladder along. It squeaks. She winces, listens, goes on.

IMA (CONT'D)
Year three. Year two. Year one.

She reaches the last drawer in the row. Beyond it is bare stone wall. She leans out, lantern held as far as her arm goes, and looks at the stone. She knocks on it. It is stone.

SOLENNE (O.S.)
(from below; unsurprised)
That ladder has been telling on children for six hundred years.

Ima looks down. SOLENNE VARR, seventy-three, in a grey robe over a nightgown, fisherman's hands folded, looking up.

IMA
Grandmother. Where's the war?

SOLENNE
Which war, little tide?

IMA
The one nobody won. It isn't here. It goes one, and then it goes wall.

Solenne opens her mouth to answer, and finds that in sixty years of climbing that ladder she has never once looked left of Year One.

SMASH CUT TO:

MAIN TITLES — NINE CONCENTRIC RINGS DRAW THEMSELVES IN LIGHT, FIVE BRIGHT AND FOUR DARK. THE NOTE UNDER THEM IS 9.1717 Hz.

ACT ONE

INT. THE DEEP ARCHIVE — THE READING PLATFORM — MORNING

SUPER: "NEPTUNE. A WEEK AFTER THE HEARING."

The lantern-lit platform at the heart of the tanks. Morning here is only a paler blue. On the reading table lies an opened message capsule, its wax seal stamped with the scales of the Balance Court, and beside it one sheet in a quick, plain hand.

Solenne reads it standing. Ima sits on the table edge, swinging her feet, eating bread.

SOLENNE
(reading)
"The Court asks the Archive for the dead of the war before the Accord. Names where there are names. We have one mark beside one clause and nobody to put to it. Adaeze."
(she turns the sheet over)
And underneath, in a worse hand: "Please. Kael."

IMA
He writes like he's hammering.

SOLENNE
He is a foundryman. He probably is.

An ARCHIVE-WARDEN approaches along the walkway: grey drifting robe, fingertips stained blue to the first knuckle. He has seen the seal and is already unhappy.

ARCHIVE-WARDEN
You've read it, then.

SOLENNE
I have. I am going to answer it, and I find I don't know where to walk. Where do we keep the war?

ARCHIVE-WARDEN
(patiently, as to a guest)
We don't. The Archive begins with the Accord. Year One, first drawer. You know that. You taught me that.

SOLENNE
I did. I was told it, and I told you, and neither of us asked what "begins" could mean in a place whose whole purpose is that nothing ever does.

ARCHIVE-WARDEN
It means there is nothing earlier. It was a war. Things were lost.

IMA
(mouth full)
Then how does the story know?

They both look at her.

IMA (CONT'D)
The old story. "Before there were twelve, there were four." That's before. If everything from before got lost, who kept that?

ARCHIVE-WARDEN
(a thin smile)
That is not a record, child. That is a bedtime story.

IMA
Grandmother's an archivist. Who told it to her?

SOLENNE
(slowly)
My grandmother.

IMA
Was she an archivist?

SOLENNE
She was Keeper of the Lower Tanks.

IMA
Who told her?

A silence. The water in the tanks moves very slightly. Solenne sits down on the bench, not heavily, but like someone who has just been handed something.

SOLENNE
(to the Warden, quietly)
It has only ever been told by archivists. I have never once heard it from a fisherman, or a baker, or a priest. Only us, and only aloud, and only to a child. I thought that was tenderness.

ARCHIVE-WARDEN
What else would it be?

SOLENNE
A filing rule.

INT. THE DEEP ARCHIVE — THE OATH STONE — DAY

A low arch at the base of the oldest tank. Set into the wall, a slab of dark green stone cut with nine lines in a script that is all curves, like water-marks on sand. Its lower edge is worn pale by twelve thousand years of hands.

Solenne stands before it with a lamp and a slate. Ima holds the chalk. The Warden hangs back in the arch.

ARCHIVE-WARDEN
Every one of us swears on that. You don't need to read it. You know it by heart.

SOLENNE
I know the sound of it by heart. I swore it at fourteen in a language nobody has spoken for nine thousand years. So did you. Did anyone ever tell you what it says?

ARCHIVE-WARDEN
"To keep, and to keep safe."

SOLENNE
That is what we say it says.

She traces the first line with one finger, lips moving. She is a scholar; this is the work she is best at, and she has never turned it on this stone.

SOLENNE (CONT'D)
(translating, slowly; Ima writes)
"What is given to the water, the water keeps."
(next line)
"What is kept shall not be lost."
(a long pause on the third)
"What comes from before the peace shall be kept."
(her finger stops)
"And shall not be found."

The chalk stops.

IMA
That's a funny thing to promise.

SOLENNE
(very quietly)
It is signed. Here, at the foot. A regent's mark. Phaedra Mir.

ARCHIVE-WARDEN
(shaken, and hiding in procedure)
The first Regent of Pisces. The founder of the Archive.

SOLENNE
The founder of the Archive wrote "shall not be found" on the door, and for twelve thousand years every one of us has put a hand on it and promised.

ACT TWO

INT. THE DEEP ARCHIVE — THE STILL TANK — DAY

The largest tank, the one where the water once made a face. Beside it, a narrower tank that the lamps do not seem to reach. Its water does not drift. Nothing in it moves at all. This is where the Drowned used to stand.

Solenne stands at the rail in front of the still tank. Ima beside her, holding her sleeve.

IMA
It's gone. You made it go.

SOLENNE
I did. I named it a lot of living people, and it couldn't bear them.
(she studies the dead water)
It said a thing at the end. "Memory so deep nothing surfaces." I took that for what it was. A boast. A Shadow's idea of itself.

IMA
Wasn't it?

SOLENNE
It was the oath, little tide. Word for word what the stone says, only without the "kept". It was not the Archive's enemy. It was the Archive doing what it promised, with nobody left to say stop.

In the great tank behind them the water rises: the slow lean of a whole sea. The lamplight shivers. A tide's idea of a face.

POSEIDON (V.O.)
(many voices of water)
Remember.

SOLENNE
(turning; and for the first time in her life she is short with him)
You said that to me in the spring. And "faster". I nearly drowned in it. You did not think to say what I was meant to be remembering, or that my own House had sworn to stop me.

The water is silent for a long moment.

POSEIDON (V.O.)
I pressed Neptune toward depth.

SOLENNE
I know you did.

POSEIDON (V.O.)
She asked me whether a thing could be kept and not carried. I said the sea does it. She was very tired. There were so many of them, and the peace was one day old.

SOLENNE
(low)
Phaedra.

POSEIDON (V.O.)
I thought it mercy. To put them where it was quiet.
(the face shifts, slow)
I did not know the quiet was listening.

Ima tugs Solenne's sleeve.

IMA
Does he mean they're here?

SOLENNE
He means that an archivist cannot throw anything away. It is the one thing we are not able to do. She couldn't destroy them, and she couldn't bear to look at them, so she did what we do.
(to the water)
Where, my lord?

POSEIDON (V.O.)
Under. Where there is no water.

The face dissolves. The level falls. On the far side of the still tank, where the wall has always been wet and dark, a line of pale stone shows: the top of a doorway that the falling water has uncovered.

INT. THE DEEP ARCHIVE — THE UNCOVERED DOOR — DAY

A low door of green stone, streaming. Beyond it, steps going down into a dark that is not blue. The Warden has brought a reel of sounding-line and two lanterns, and has stopped being unhappy because he is frightened instead.

ARCHIVE-WARDEN
There is no lower level. I have the plans.

SOLENNE
You have the plans from Year One.

She ties the sounding-line around her waist with a fisherman's knot, fast, without looking. She gives the reel to the Warden. Then she changes her mind, takes it back, and puts it into Ima's two hands.

SOLENNE (CONT'D)
You, I think.

IMA
(solemn)
What do I do?

SOLENNE
Hold the other end.

Ima grips the reel as if it were the whole of Neptune. Solenne lifts her lantern and puts one foot on the first step.

IMA
Wait.

Solenne stops.

IMA (CONT'D)
The Knight boy says you have to do it first. Before the big thing. Say the cost.

The Warden looks at the child as if she has spoken in the script on the stone. Solenne does not laugh. She considers it, standing on the step with the water running past her feet.

SOLENNE
If I go down, I will know. I can't put it back in the tank after that. And everything I have loved about this place for sixty years, the patience of it, the way it never hurries, will turn out to have been one tired woman's promise not to look.
(a breath)
And you will have a grandmother who broke her oath.

IMA
(after thinking about it properly)
It's a bad oath.

SOLENNE
Yes. That is the cost of that.

She goes down.

ACT THREE

INT. THE DRY HALL — CONTINUOUS

Solenne's lantern comes down the last step into a place with no water in it at all.

It is as large as the hall above, and it is dry. The air is still and smells of nothing. Instead of tanks there are shelves: plain grey stone, floor to roof, running away in aisles beyond the reach of the light. After a lifetime of blue, the lantern's small yellow circle looks like another country.

Every shelf carries brass label-holders, neatly riveted, one to each hand's width. Every label-holder has a card in it.

Solenne walks to the nearest. Reads.

SOLENNE
(aloud; habit)
"Treaty drafts, unsigned. Mercury delegation."

The next.

SOLENNE (CONT'D)
"Fleet musters. Jupiter."
"Supply tallies. Ceres, third winter."

She walks faster. It is all here: the war, shelved, labelled, in order, in a hand she almost recognises as the ancestor of her own. Orders. Maps. Rations. Signals. Twelve Houses' worth of a war, kept and not found.

SOLENNE (CONT'D)
(to herself; an archivist's despair)
Quartermasters. She kept the quartermasters.

She turns down another aisle, and another. Musters. Tallies. Weights of grain. No names. Numbers of ships, never of crews.

The sounding-line at her waist goes slack, then taut, then slack: someone above has let out a great deal of it very quickly.

SOLENNE (CONT'D)
(sharply, up the stair)
Ima?

No answer. Then, small and near, from two aisles over:

IMA (O.S.)
I'm holding it. I just brought it with me.

Solenne closes her eyes for one second. Then she follows the line through the shelves, hand over hand.

INT. THE DRY HALL — THE FAR AISLE — CONTINUOUS

She finds Ima standing in the mouth of an aisle narrower than the others, the reel hugged against her chest, her own lantern on the floor. The child is not frightened. She is looking up.

IMA
Grandmother. These ones forgot their cards.

Solenne raises her lantern.

The shelves here are the same grey stone. The brass holders are the same, riveted at the same hand's width, floor to roof. Every one of them is empty.

And on the shelves, in rows, one to each empty holder: small things. A spoon, worn thin on one side. A child's shoe. A ring too small for a man. A clay whistle. A folded square of cloth with one corner darned. A key. A comb with three teeth gone.

Solenne does not touch anything. She walks a little way in. The aisle does not end. The lantern finds shelf after shelf, object after object, holder after empty holder, and beyond the light there is only more aisle.

IMA (CONT'D)
(whispering now)
Whose are they?

SOLENNE
(hardly a voice)
That is what is missing, little tide. That is the only thing that is missing.

She lifts the lantern as high as her arm will go. It makes no difference. The shelf with no labels goes on into the dark, further than anyone has ever counted.

CUT TO BLACK.

TAG

INT. THE BALANCE COURT — A CUSTODY ROOM — NIGHT

The plain room, the window with no bars. OSSARA at the table. The paper lies blank side up: the number 1, and nothing after it.

The door opens. ADAEZE, glasses on her forehead, a message capsule in her hand, its seal stamped with a wave.

ADAEZE
The Court voted. You may look for the first one. Under watch. You will not hold a pen.

OSSARA
And you? How did you vote?

ADAEZE
The same way. Those are two different facts and I'd like you to know I know it.

She sets the capsule on the table.

ADAEZE (CONT'D)
From Neptune. They have found where the dead are kept.

OSSARA
(very still)
And who they are?

ADAEZE
A spoon. A shoe. A whistle.
(she turns to go)
Nobody wrote who.

Ossara looks down at the page. The number 1. He does not pick anything up.

CLOSING SEQUENCE — THE NINE RINGS FADE. A SINGLE LOW TONE, 9.1717 Hz, HOLDS UNDER THE CREDITS.

THE END OF EPISODE 103 (DRAFT 1 SHORT)
